 ## Overview
-Morton Code Encoding/Decoding is a method to rearrange pixels or voxels from an $n$-dimensional regular grid into a linear 1D form using the Morton Space Filling Curve (MSFC), also known as the Z-order curve [S1][S8, unofficial]. It converts multi-dimensional integer coordinates into a single 1D Morton index by interleaving their binary representations, thereby preserving spatial locality such that geometrically adjacent points have close linear indices [S1][S8, unofficial]. This ordering reduces the number of disk seek operations required to access subsets of data with similar properties, as indices are sorted by value and similar data points are placed contiguously in memory or on disk [S5, unofficial]. It builds on the parent principle of space-filling curves to optimize cache performance [1797-spatial-indexing-and-ordering.md].
+Morton Code Encoding/Decoding is a method to rearrange pixels or voxels from an $n$-dimensional regular grid into a linear 1D form using the Morton Space Filling Curve (MSFC), also known as the Z-order curve [S1]. It converts multi-dimensional integer coordinates into a single 1D Morton index by interleaving their binary representations, thereby preserving spatial locality such that geometrically adjacent points have close linear indices [S1][S8, unofficial]. This ordering reduces the number of disk seek operations required to access subsets of data with similar properties, as indices are sorted by value and similar data points are placed contiguously in memory or on disk [S5, unofficial]. It builds on the parent principle of space-filling curves to optimize cache performance [G].
 
